1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,442
Saturday morning. Dave has two prized possessions:

2
00:00:03,442 --> 00:00:05,645
a sensible used car, and a model

3
00:00:05,645 --> 00:00:07,848
rocket he built with his nephew.

4
00:00:08,128 --> 00:00:09,253
Dave has an idea.

5
00:00:09,253 --> 00:00:11,240
What if the car and the rocket

6
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,233
were one thing?

7
00:00:12,233 --> 00:00:13,690
He gets the duct tape.

8
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:16,829
Linda, from the porch, asks what problem

9
00:00:16,829 --> 00:00:19,117
this solves. Dave says: synergy.

10
00:00:19,117 --> 00:00:20,761
Linda goes back inside.

11
00:00:21,041 --> 00:00:22,581
Dave's idea is a joke.

12
00:00:22,581 --> 00:00:24,892
But on Tuesday night, the boss of

13
00:00:24,892 --> 00:00:27,272
a rocket company and a car company

14
00:00:27,272 --> 00:00:28,883
said something similar.

15
00:00:28,883 --> 00:00:31,123
About companies worth trillions.

16
00:00:31,403 --> 00:00:34,569
SpaceX and Tesla have formed special committees

17
00:00:34,569 --> 00:00:35,848
to look at merging.

18
00:00:35,848 --> 00:00:38,475
Put the two together at today's prices,

19
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:40,900
and you get roughly three point five

20
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,045
trillion dollars.

21
00:00:42,325 --> 00:00:45,349
So today's question: why would anyone glue

22
00:00:45,349 --> 00:00:47,797
a rocket company to a car company,

23
00:00:47,797 --> 00:00:50,605
who decides, and who gets nervous about

24
00:00:50,605 --> 00:00:51,685
it? Facts only.

25
00:00:51,685 --> 00:00:53,485
Stick figures, obviously.

26
00:00:53,765 --> 00:00:55,665
Tuesday, October twentieth.

27
00:00:55,665 --> 00:00:58,551
SpaceX reports its third-quarter results.

28
00:00:58,551 --> 00:01:01,226
On the call with investors, the merger

29
00:01:01,226 --> 00:01:02,493
question comes up.

30
00:01:02,773 --> 00:01:05,581
The man running both companies, shown here

31
00:01:05,581 --> 00:01:07,987
as a generic stick figure, said both

32
00:01:07,987 --> 00:01:11,129
boards had formed special committees to discuss

33
00:01:11,129 --> 00:01:11,730
the idea.

34
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:14,185
Then he added, quote: Because I'm a

35
00:01:14,185 --> 00:01:16,671
conflicted party, I can't actually be in

36
00:01:16,671 --> 00:01:19,281
the discussions of the special committees.

37
00:01:19,561 --> 00:01:20,859
Which makes sense.

38
00:01:20,859 --> 00:01:21,940
He runs SpaceX.

39
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:22,949
He runs Tesla.

40
00:01:22,949 --> 00:01:25,689
If he negotiated, he would be haggling

41
00:01:25,689 --> 00:01:26,626
with himself.

42
00:01:26,906 --> 00:01:29,760
So two rooms of independent directors talk

43
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,206
instead. He called it, quote, an odd

44
00:01:32,206 --> 00:01:34,856
kabuki dance where two groups of people

45
00:01:34,856 --> 00:01:35,671
are talking.

46
00:01:35,951 --> 00:01:37,393
Does he like the idea?

47
00:01:37,393 --> 00:01:39,688
Asked directly, he said: Do I think

48
00:01:39,688 --> 00:01:41,261
it would be a good idea?

49
00:01:41,261 --> 00:01:41,523
Yes.

50
00:01:41,803 --> 00:01:44,130
But he also warned against treating it

51
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:46,274
as done. Calling a deal certain, he

52
00:01:46,274 --> 00:01:48,111
said, would be overstating it.

53
00:01:48,391 --> 00:01:51,899
TechCrunch reports the committees haven't agreed on

54
00:01:51,899 --> 00:01:52,862
any terms yet.

55
00:01:52,862 --> 00:01:55,063
No price, no structure, no date.

56
00:01:55,063 --> 00:01:57,264
Just two rooms and a whiteboard.

57
00:01:57,544 --> 00:01:59,719
Wall Street got excited anyway.

58
00:01:59,719 --> 00:02:02,385
SpaceX stock closed Tuesday at about a

59
00:02:02,385 --> 00:02:04,350
hundred forty-eight dollars.

60
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:07,508
Before markets opened Wednesday, it was about

61
00:02:07,508 --> 00:02:09,051
a hundred fifty-eight.

62
00:02:09,331 --> 00:02:11,828
That's a jump of about seven percent,

63
00:02:11,828 --> 00:02:14,663
partly the merger talk, partly very strong

64
00:02:14,663 --> 00:02:15,878
quarterly results.

65
00:02:16,158 --> 00:02:19,087
Let's meet the two companies, with numbers.

66
00:02:19,087 --> 00:02:21,608
First, SpaceX. It went public in June

67
00:02:21,608 --> 00:02:24,402
at a hundred thirty-five dollars a share.

68
00:02:24,682 --> 00:02:26,832
Today the market values it at more

69
00:02:26,832 --> 00:02:28,476
than two trillion dollars.

70
00:02:28,476 --> 00:02:30,942
That's bigger than the whole economy of

71
00:02:30,942 --> 00:02:31,890
most countries.

72
00:02:32,170 --> 00:02:35,126
According to CBS News, citing World Bank

73
00:02:35,126 --> 00:02:38,451
data, only nineteen countries have an economy

74
00:02:38,451 --> 00:02:40,963
above one trillion dollars a year.

75
00:02:41,243 --> 00:02:44,716
SpaceX's third quarter: revenue of fourteen point

76
00:02:44,716 --> 00:02:46,133
six billion dollars.

77
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:48,968
That's up a hundred sixty-one percent on

78
00:02:48,968 --> 00:02:50,031
a year earlier.

79
00:02:50,311 --> 00:02:51,924
But here's the surprise.

80
00:02:51,924 --> 00:02:54,544
The biggest slice is no longer rockets,

81
00:02:54,544 --> 00:02:57,098
and it isn't Starlink internet either.

82
00:02:57,098 --> 00:02:59,046
It's artificial intelligence.

83
00:02:59,326 --> 00:03:02,266
Earlier this year SpaceX bought xAI, the

84
00:03:02,266 --> 00:03:06,383
artificial intelligence company behind the Grok chatbot.

85
00:03:06,383 --> 00:03:09,543
That unit is now called Super Intelligence.

86
00:03:09,823 --> 00:03:12,943
Fortune reports AI brought in seven point

87
00:03:12,943 --> 00:03:15,834
five six billion dollars this quarter.

88
00:03:15,834 --> 00:03:19,334
Starlink and connectivity: five point two five

89
00:03:19,334 --> 00:03:22,605
billion. Rockets and space: one point seven

90
00:03:22,605 --> 00:03:23,062
seven.

91
00:03:23,342 --> 00:03:26,715
Net profit: four point four billion dollars.

92
00:03:26,715 --> 00:03:30,242
Spending on equipment, mostly for AI: nineteen

93
00:03:30,242 --> 00:03:31,622
point six billion.

94
00:03:31,622 --> 00:03:32,848
In three months.

95
00:03:33,128 --> 00:03:35,285
Now Tesla. Worth about one and a

96
00:03:35,285 --> 00:03:36,767
half trillion dollars.

97
00:03:36,767 --> 00:03:39,530
It reported its own results on Wednesday.

98
00:03:39,810 --> 00:03:43,101
Revenue: twenty-eight point zero five billion.

99
00:03:43,101 --> 00:03:47,393
Basically flat. Deliveries: four hundred eighty-six thousand

100
00:03:47,393 --> 00:03:49,038
cars, down two percent.

101
00:03:49,318 --> 00:03:52,400
And net profit: seven hundred eighty-seven million

102
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:55,297
dollars. That's forty-three percent less than a

103
00:03:55,297 --> 00:03:56,098
year earlier.

104
00:03:56,378 --> 00:03:58,931
Tesla says battery supply was the limit

105
00:03:58,931 --> 00:04:00,960
on how many cars it could make,

106
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:03,120
and that its order backlog is the

107
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:04,560
biggest in four years.

108
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,678
So, side by side: one company growing

109
00:04:07,678 --> 00:04:10,668
at rocket speed, one making less profit

110
00:04:10,668 --> 00:04:13,659
than last year but promising robots and

111
00:04:13,659 --> 00:04:14,426
robotaxis.

112
00:04:14,706 --> 00:04:17,156
Okay. Why would anyone want to combine

113
00:04:17,156 --> 00:04:19,800
them? Supporters give three main reasons.

114
00:04:20,080 --> 00:04:22,698
Reason one: they already work together.

115
00:04:22,698 --> 00:04:25,450
The two companies run a joint chip-making

116
00:04:25,450 --> 00:04:26,994
project called Terafab.

117
00:04:27,274 --> 00:04:30,624
SpaceX also has a plan called Starmind,

118
00:04:30,624 --> 00:04:33,115
for AI data centers in orbit.

119
00:04:33,115 --> 00:04:36,035
And Tesla's cars and robots run on

120
00:04:36,035 --> 00:04:36,636
AI too.

121
00:04:36,916 --> 00:04:39,140
The CEO said there's a lot to

122
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:42,207
be gained from cooperating on AI, chips,

123
00:04:42,207 --> 00:04:44,201
robotics and connectivity.

124
00:04:44,201 --> 00:04:47,115
One company, one plan, fewer meetings.

125
00:04:47,395 --> 00:04:50,015
Reason two: money flowing between them.

126
00:04:50,015 --> 00:04:52,903
Fortune reports Tesla did over five hundred

127
00:04:52,903 --> 00:04:57,067
seventy-three million dollars of business with Musk-controlled

128
00:04:57,067 --> 00:04:58,411
companies last year.

129
00:04:58,691 --> 00:05:02,105
For example, about four hundred thirty million

130
00:05:02,105 --> 00:05:05,074
in Tesla Megapack batteries sold to xAI,

131
00:05:05,074 --> 00:05:08,636
and a hundred forty-three million in Cybertrucks

132
00:05:08,636 --> 00:05:09,750
sold to SpaceX.

133
00:05:10,030 --> 00:05:13,272
Tesla's audit committee judged those deals fair,

134
00:05:13,272 --> 00:05:14,353
at arm's length.

135
00:05:14,353 --> 00:05:16,718
But every deal like that raises the

136
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:19,623
question: is everyone getting a fair price?

137
00:05:19,903 --> 00:05:22,714
One merged company would make those questions

138
00:05:22,714 --> 00:05:25,338
disappear, because it would just be paying

139
00:05:25,338 --> 00:05:25,775
itself.

140
00:05:26,055 --> 00:05:28,942
Reason three: Wall Street seems to expect

141
00:05:28,942 --> 00:05:31,759
it. Barclays analyst Dan Levy wrote that

142
00:05:31,759 --> 00:05:34,998
a tie-up has increasingly become the consensus

143
00:05:34,998 --> 00:05:35,843
expectation.

144
00:05:36,123 --> 00:05:38,335
Analyst Gene Munster went further.

145
00:05:38,335 --> 00:05:40,157
He puts the chance of a deal

146
00:05:40,157 --> 00:05:42,759
within two years at ninety-five percent.

147
00:05:43,039 --> 00:05:46,773
Dave, meanwhile, rates his car-rocket at ninety-five

148
00:05:46,773 --> 00:05:49,861
percent too. Ninety-five percent duct tape.

149
00:05:50,141 --> 00:05:51,693
So who actually decides?

150
00:05:51,693 --> 00:05:53,892
This is where it gets interesting.

151
00:05:53,892 --> 00:05:55,509
And it's all about votes.

152
00:05:55,789 --> 00:05:57,862
At SpaceX, the boss owns a bit

153
00:05:57,862 --> 00:05:59,452
under half the company.

154
00:05:59,452 --> 00:06:02,079
But his special shares carry ten votes

155
00:06:02,079 --> 00:06:02,424
each.

156
00:06:02,704 --> 00:06:05,812
Fortune and Barclays put his voting power

157
00:06:05,812 --> 00:06:08,542
at SpaceX around eighty-two percent.

158
00:06:08,542 --> 00:06:10,967
So SpaceX's side of any vote is,

159
00:06:10,967 --> 00:06:12,711
let's say, predictable.

160
00:06:12,991 --> 00:06:14,287
Tesla is different.

161
00:06:14,287 --> 00:06:16,673
He owns about twenty percent of it.

162
00:06:16,673 --> 00:06:18,991
And a merger would need a majority

163
00:06:18,991 --> 00:06:20,355
of all Tesla shares.

164
00:06:20,355 --> 00:06:22,264
Not just the ones that vote.

165
00:06:22,544 --> 00:06:24,643
He called that, quote, quite a high

166
00:06:24,643 --> 00:06:27,641
hurdle. Because shareholders who don't vote count,

167
00:06:27,641 --> 00:06:28,780
in effect, as a no.

168
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:30,883
This has happened before.

169
00:06:30,883 --> 00:06:34,966
In November twenty twenty-five, Tesla shareholders voted

170
00:06:34,966 --> 00:06:36,424
on investing in xAI.

171
00:06:36,704 --> 00:06:39,222
More shares voted for than against.

172
00:06:39,222 --> 00:06:41,741
About one point oh six billion yes,

173
00:06:41,741 --> 00:06:44,043
nine hundred sixteen million no.

174
00:06:44,323 --> 00:06:47,691
But more than four hundred seventy-three million

175
00:06:47,691 --> 00:06:51,480
abstained. Under Tesla's rules, abstentions counted as

176
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:53,304
no. So the measure failed.

177
00:06:53,584 --> 00:06:56,950
Two months later, Tesla put two billion

178
00:06:56,950 --> 00:06:59,022
dollars into xAI anyway.

179
00:06:59,022 --> 00:07:02,043
Days after that, SpaceX bought xAI.

180
00:07:02,323 --> 00:07:04,202
So how might a deal work?

181
00:07:04,202 --> 00:07:07,284
Barclays expects SpaceX to buy Tesla with

182
00:07:07,284 --> 00:07:08,411
its own shares.

183
00:07:08,411 --> 00:07:11,568
Roughly zero point seven SpaceX shares for

184
00:07:11,568 --> 00:07:14,349
each Tesla share, before any premium.

185
00:07:14,629 --> 00:07:17,023
By Barclays' math, the boss would own

186
00:07:17,023 --> 00:07:20,322
about thirty-seven percent of the combined company,

187
00:07:20,322 --> 00:07:23,039
with about fifty-six percent of the votes.

188
00:07:23,319 --> 00:07:26,569
Barclays sees Tesla's annual meeting as the

189
00:07:26,569 --> 00:07:28,383
likely place for a vote.

190
00:07:28,383 --> 00:07:31,029
Under SEC rules, Tesla must hold it

191
00:07:31,029 --> 00:07:32,389
by December sixth.

192
00:07:32,669 --> 00:07:34,921
Dave tries the same idea at home.

193
00:07:34,921 --> 00:07:37,446
He announces a family vote on merging

194
00:07:37,446 --> 00:07:39,015
the car and the rocket.

195
00:07:39,295 --> 00:07:40,867
The nephew votes yes.

196
00:07:40,867 --> 00:07:44,312
Linda abstains. Under Tesla rules, Dave loses.

197
00:07:44,592 --> 00:07:46,221
So what do people think?

198
00:07:46,221 --> 00:07:48,462
Depending on who you ask, this is

199
00:07:48,462 --> 00:07:51,110
either smart housekeeping or a big test

200
00:07:51,110 --> 00:07:52,400
of who's in charge.

201
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,955
Supporters say the companies already share chips,

202
00:07:55,955 --> 00:07:58,228
AI and money, so combining them is

203
00:07:58,228 --> 00:07:59,966
simply honest bookkeeping.

204
00:08:00,246 --> 00:08:02,615
Munster put it plainly on X: Believe

205
00:08:02,615 --> 00:08:04,984
me, shareholders would support this.

206
00:08:05,264 --> 00:08:07,138
Critics focus on control.

207
00:08:07,138 --> 00:08:10,362
In May, big public pension funds, including

208
00:08:10,362 --> 00:08:13,512
California's CalPERS and the New York City

209
00:08:13,512 --> 00:08:16,811
and State comptrollers, wrote a letter about

210
00:08:16,811 --> 00:08:17,335
SpaceX.

211
00:08:17,615 --> 00:08:19,604
They said its setup would be the

212
00:08:19,604 --> 00:08:23,145
most management-favorable governance ever brought to U.S.

213
00:08:23,145 --> 00:08:24,512
markets at this scale.

214
00:08:24,792 --> 00:08:27,867
Fortune notes SpaceX skips some usual rules,

215
00:08:27,867 --> 00:08:31,641
like needing a majority-independent board, because one

216
00:08:31,641 --> 00:08:34,017
person controls most of the votes.

217
00:08:34,297 --> 00:08:36,987
Their worry: after a merger, Tesla's outside

218
00:08:36,987 --> 00:08:39,249
shareholders would have less of a say

219
00:08:39,249 --> 00:08:40,411
than they do today.

220
00:08:40,691 --> 00:08:43,625
Supporters answer that every big decision still

221
00:08:43,625 --> 00:08:46,933
goes through independent committees and a shareholder

222
00:08:46,933 --> 00:08:49,555
vote. That's exactly what's happening now.

223
00:08:49,835 --> 00:08:52,507
And investors who disagree can vote no,

224
00:08:52,507 --> 00:08:54,562
or sell. Which is also how the

225
00:08:54,562 --> 00:08:55,864
stock market works.

226
00:08:56,144 --> 00:08:57,794
We're not picking a side here.

227
00:08:57,794 --> 00:08:59,113
We just count the facts.

228
00:08:59,393 --> 00:09:01,055
So does any of this matter if

229
00:09:01,055 --> 00:09:02,487
you're not a billionaire?

230
00:09:02,487 --> 00:09:03,289
A little, yes.

231
00:09:03,569 --> 00:09:06,594
Lots of ordinary people own both companies

232
00:09:06,594 --> 00:09:09,691
without knowing it, through index funds and

233
00:09:09,691 --> 00:09:11,995
pension plans. Tesla is in the S

234
00:09:11,995 --> 00:09:13,363
and P five hundred.

235
00:09:13,643 --> 00:09:15,750
So if you have a retirement account,

236
00:09:15,750 --> 00:09:17,799
there's a fair chance a small piece

237
00:09:17,799 --> 00:09:19,554
of you will be voting on this,

238
00:09:19,554 --> 00:09:20,608
through your fund.

239
00:09:20,888 --> 00:09:24,012
For Tesla drivers, nothing changes tomorrow.

240
00:09:24,012 --> 00:09:25,574
Your car still drives.

241
00:09:25,574 --> 00:09:27,137
It still does not fly.

242
00:09:27,417 --> 00:09:29,483
And there's the AI angle.

243
00:09:29,483 --> 00:09:32,707
If AI is now SpaceX's biggest business,

244
00:09:32,707 --> 00:09:35,435
a merger would make one giant bet

245
00:09:35,435 --> 00:09:37,832
on AI. Bets can win, and they

246
00:09:37,832 --> 00:09:38,576
can lose.

247
00:09:38,856 --> 00:09:41,327
By the way, Tesla's own results call

248
00:09:41,327 --> 00:09:43,660
on Wednesday had a rule: no merger

249
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:46,817
questions. The head of investor relations said

250
00:09:46,817 --> 00:09:48,327
so right at the start.

251
00:09:48,607 --> 00:09:50,688
When one came up anyway, the CEO

252
00:09:50,688 --> 00:09:53,028
said: I think I said everything that

253
00:09:53,028 --> 00:09:55,953
should be said yesterday, tap dancing through

254
00:09:55,953 --> 00:09:57,123
a minefield there.

255
00:09:57,403 --> 00:10:00,199
Tesla's stock slipped about two percent after

256
00:10:00,199 --> 00:10:02,808
hours on Wednesday, to about three hundred

257
00:10:02,808 --> 00:10:04,174
seventy-eight dollars.

258
00:10:04,454 --> 00:10:05,432
So what's next?

259
00:10:05,432 --> 00:10:06,865
Three things to watch.

260
00:10:07,145 --> 00:10:09,647
One: whether the two committees agree on

261
00:10:09,647 --> 00:10:11,524
a price. Right now there is no

262
00:10:11,524 --> 00:10:12,650
deal on the table.

263
00:10:12,930 --> 00:10:15,981
Two: Tesla's annual meeting, due by December

264
00:10:15,981 --> 00:10:19,171
sixth. Barclays thinks that's the likely stage

265
00:10:19,171 --> 00:10:19,934
for a vote.

266
00:10:20,214 --> 00:10:23,916
Three: whether enough Tesla shareholders actually show

267
00:10:23,916 --> 00:10:24,945
up and say yes.

268
00:10:24,945 --> 00:10:27,070
Remember, silence counts as no.

269
00:10:27,350 --> 00:10:30,063
Quick recap. SpaceX and Tesla have formed

270
00:10:30,063 --> 00:10:32,444
committees to look at merging into a

271
00:10:32,444 --> 00:10:34,892
company worth around three and a half

272
00:10:34,892 --> 00:10:36,017
trillion dollars.

273
00:10:36,297 --> 00:10:39,142
Supporters say they already share chips, AI

274
00:10:39,142 --> 00:10:41,723
and money. Critics say one person would

275
00:10:41,723 --> 00:10:43,642
control too much of the vote.

276
00:10:43,922 --> 00:10:46,727
And nothing happens unless a majority of

277
00:10:46,727 --> 00:10:48,481
all Tesla shares say yes.

278
00:10:48,761 --> 00:10:51,343
Back at Dave's house, the car-rocket is

279
00:10:51,343 --> 00:10:53,131
finished. He turns the key.

280
00:10:53,131 --> 00:10:56,110
Nothing happens. Then the rocket engine rolls

281
00:10:56,110 --> 00:10:58,229
off and lands in the flower bed.

282
00:10:58,509 --> 00:11:00,991
Linda says some mergers need a special

283
00:11:00,991 --> 00:11:03,341
committee. Dave says he'll form one.

284
00:11:03,341 --> 00:11:05,888
It's just him, in two different chairs.

285
00:11:06,168 --> 00:11:08,489
Your turn: should one person run two

286
00:11:08,489 --> 00:11:11,003
giant companies at once, or should they

287
00:11:11,003 --> 00:11:12,034
just become one?

288
00:11:12,034 --> 00:11:13,582
Tell us in the comments.

289
00:11:13,862 --> 00:11:16,610
And if calm stick figures explaining loud

290
00:11:16,610 --> 00:11:19,158
news is your kind of thing, subscribe.

291
00:11:19,158 --> 00:11:20,632
No duct tape required.

